WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
How can two purple-flowered pea plants have a white-flowered offspring?

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.700
Picture two purple-flowered parents, and between them, one offspring with white

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:15.060
flowers. Nothing about the parents’ visible color seems to explain it.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.940
The answer is hidden inside what each parent carries.

5
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:24.123
Look inside one parent.

6
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:28.683
Its flower-color gene comes in versions called alleles.

7
00:00:29.843 --> 00:00:32.703
This plant carries a purple allele and a white allele.

8
00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:36.723
P is dominant, so purple shows.

9
00:00:38.063 --> 00:00:43.563
p is recessive. It is hidden from view, not damaged or erased.

10
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.583
The allele pair a plant carries is its genotype.

11
00:00:48.743 --> 00:00:51.223
The flower color that shows is its phenotype.

12
00:00:52.523 --> 00:00:54.923
This parent’s genotype is Pp.

13
00:00:56.323 --> 00:00:57.623
Its phenotype is purple.

14
00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:03.963
A purple plant can carry a hidden p allele, so appearance alone cannot reveal its

15
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:08.383
pair. Carried information and the visible trait can differ.

16
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:09.383
Carried information and the visible trait can differ.

17
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.907
Before offspring form, the pair separates.

18
00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:15.887
Each sex cell carries one allele from the parent.

19
00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:21.367
Watch P enter one teal path and p enter another.

20
00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:24.207
The other parent does the same.

21
00:01:25.527 --> 00:01:28.027
Either parent can pass the hidden p onward.

22
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:32.117
A Punnett square organizes those possible meetings.

23
00:01:33.477 --> 00:01:36.077
One parent’s sex cells settle across the top.

24
00:01:37.337 --> 00:01:38.957
The other’s settle down the side.

25
00:01:40.237 --> 00:01:42.517
Follow a teal path from each edge into a box.

26
00:01:43.777 --> 00:01:48.037
The two arriving alleles become one possible genotype for an offspring.

27
00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:52.857
Both parents’ contributions stay separate and easy to trace.

28
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.710
Three boxes now hold possible allele pairs.

29
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.930
The last box receives p from each parent.

30
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.810
What genotype and flower color should appear there?

31
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.250
Keep your answer in mind while the paths move.

32
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.720
Both p alleles meet.

33
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.040
The genotype is pp, so the white phenotype appears.

34
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.920
The white color never showed in the parents’ flowers.

35
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.580
It was carried as a hidden allele in each parent, then joined in this offspring.

36
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:33.153
Now read the whole grid.

37
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:36.313
The allele pairs predict genotypes.

38
00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:39.773
The flowers translate them into phenotypes.

39
00:02:41.173 --> 00:02:43.753
Most boxes show purple, while one shows white.

40
00:02:45.113 --> 00:02:48.293
Each box describes a possible outcome for a new offspring.

41
00:02:49.593 --> 00:02:52.233
The grid does not schedule what must happen next.

42
00:02:53.283 --> 00:02:55.003
This model has a boundary.

43
00:02:56.263 --> 00:03:01.163
It follows one gene, two alleles, one of them dominant, and known parent genotypes.

44
00:03:02.543 --> 00:03:06.623
Within that scope, it links what parents carry to what offspring may inherit.

45
00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:09.383
Real groups can still vary.

46
00:03:10.643 --> 00:03:15.583
For one-gene crosses, Punnett squares predict chances, not exact offspring counts.
